bug_descriptions |bug_id, line_num, content|
    seed_jit_any_typed_tuple_len_returns_minus_one_2026-11-27, 0, "Repro (seed 20ce479a53519bab = len-proof fix, release/1.0.0, aarch64, JIT; interpreter correct): fn count(x) -> i64: x.len(); val t = (7, 40, 3); val a: any = t; a.len() and count(t) both return -1 on the JIT (interpreter 3); arrays, text and dicts are fine. Root: an erased .len() lowers to the bare len call, which the Cranelift backend inlines through inline_runtime_len_value (src/compiler_rust/compiler/src/codegen/instr/helpers.rs:53). Its fast path decodes heap tags 1 (string), 2 (array) and 3 (dict) and answers -1 for anything else, including the Rust runtime's RuntimeTuple (HeapObjectType::Tuple = 0x04). Tag 4 cannot be decoded inline: in the C core it is RT_VALUE_HEAP_ENUM (C tuples are arrays with RT_CORE_ARRAY_FLAG_TUPLE). The LLVM len fast path (codegen/llvm/functions/calls.rs ~500) also decodes only strings and arrays; native builds link the C core, where a tuple is an array, so it is not affected. Found in review of c3a73002cb4 (Fable)."

